> I'm trying to solve a problem that also seems to require a generalized Poisson. I'm trying to fit a model of a count of events as a function of two variables, but unsuccessfully so far. I'm following the parameterization of the generalized poisson presented here http://www.stata-journal.com/sjpdf.html?articlenum=st0279. Please give me some advice for my code if possible. Thanks!
>
> # Generalized poisson
> stancode <- "functions {
>   real generalized_poisson(int n, real theta, real lambda) {
>     return log(theta)
>     + (n - 1) * log(theta + n * lambda)
>     - lgamma(n + 1)
>     - n * lambda
>     - theta;
>   }
> }

You don't really need a function for this, and if you were to write one, you would probably want to input an integer array for n and vectors for theta and lambda. Also, you would want to drop the lgamma(n + 1) term, which is expensive but constant.

> data {
>   int<lower=0> N;
>   int No_outbreaks[N];
>   real Score_surv100_c[N];
>   real windeg_c[N];
> }
> parameters {
>   vector[N] theta;
>   real<lower=-1, upper=1> lambda;

This is not as tight a constraint as the one required by the article. You need something like

real<lower=fmax(-1.0, max(theta) / 4), upper = 1> lambda;

> model {
>   vector[N] lambdastar;
>   theta[N] ~ exponential(1);
>   lambda ~ normal(0,1);
>   bsi ~ normal( 0 , 1 );
>   bi ~ normal( 0 , 1 );
>   bs ~ normal( 0 , 1 );
>   a ~ normal( 0 , 100 );
>
>   for (i in 1:N){
>   lamdastar[i] = theta[i]/(1-lambda)
>   }

You don't need a loop (but if you had one, you would need a semi-colon); just do

lambdastar = theta / (1 - lambda);

>   for (i in 1:N){
>   log(lamdastar[i]) = a + bs * Score_surv100_c[i] + bi * windeg_c[i] + bsi * Score_surv100_c[i] * windeg_c[i];
>   }

This BUGS-style statement won't work at all. You can't assign to log(lambdastar[i]). What I think you are trying to do is to define the mean as a function of covariances, in which case theta would be a local parameter rather than a primitive parameter. If so, then you are better off passing in a matrix of covariates rather than hard-coding it to a particular set of covariate vectors. All together, your Stan program would be

data {
  int<lower=0> N;
  int<lower=0> K;
  matrix[N, K] X;
  vector<lower=0>[N] y;
}
transformed data {
  vector[N] ym1 = y - 1;
}
parameters {
  vector[K] beta;
  real<lower=fmax(-1.0, max(exp(X * beta)) / 4), upper = 1> lambda;
}
model {
  vector[N] eta = X * beta;
  vector[N] theta = exp(eta) * (1 - lambda);
  vector[N] theta_lambda_y = theta + lambda * y;

  target += eta + log1m(lambda) + ym1 .* log(theta_lambda_y) - theta_lambda_y;
  // prior on beta
  // possibly a prior on lambda, although you may have to truncate
}

Ben

