Hello,

The model below imputes missing data from a multivariate

normal distribution (for some variable after applying a logit transform).

This works well in most cases, but for some data some chains do not manage

to estimates the covariance matrix and means of the mvn.

At first I thought the problem might be that I have highly correlated data that result

in a non-invertible covariance martrix. While I indeed begin to observe

problems when the determinant of the cov matrix of the complete data drops from

0.14 to 0.07, the determinant never becomes smaller than 0.006 and some chains always

manage to estimate the covariance matrix and means of the mvn.

So stan definitively can estimate those parameters, just not consistently

with each chain with the model below.

It would be nice if anybody could look at the model code below and see if

I am doing something wrong.

Best - Guido

data {
  int<lower = 1> N;      # total N
  int<lower = 1> D;      # number DVs
  matrix[N,D] y;         # outcome seperately for DVs
  int<lower=0, upper=1> logit_transf[D]; # 1 if logit transform is to be applied
  int missings[N,D];
  int nmiss[D];
}

transformed data{
  matrix[N,D] y_normal;
  int missing_counter[N,D];
  int missingL[N,D];
  int imp_idx_start[D];
  int imp_idx_end[D];
  {
    int mc;
    mc = 0;
    for(v in 1:D) for (n in 1:N) {
      missingL[n,v] =  missings[n,v] > 0 ? 1 : 0;
      mc = missingL[n,v] ? mc+1 : mc;
      missing_counter[n,v] =  missingL[n,v] ? mc : 0;
      y_normal[n,v] = missingL[n,v] ? 0 : (logit_transf[v] ? logit(y[n,v]) : y[n,v]);
    }
  }
}

parameters {
  cholesky_factor_corr[D] i_L_Omega; // correlation matrix
  vector[D] i_mu;
  real imps[sum(nmiss)];
}

model {
  row_vector[D] y_normal_i[N];
  //matrix[N,D] y_i;

  i_mu ~ normal(0,3);
  i_L_Omega ~ lkj_corr_cholesky(2);
  imps ~ normal(0,2);

  for (v in 1:D) {
    // no missing data
    if (nmiss[v] == 0) {
      for (n in 1:N) {
        y_normal_i[n,v] = y_normal[n,v];
      }
    // missings data
    } else {
      // imputation on unbounded (normally distributed) scale
      for (n in 1:N) {
        y_normal_i[n,v] = missingL[n,v] ? imps[missing_counter[n,v]] : y_normal[n,v];
      }
    }
  }
  // log posterior for imputations
  y_normal_i ~ multi_normal_cholesky(i_mu, i_L_Omega);
}

